  "vulnerability_information": "### Summary\n\nAt `lib/ws.c:503`, `ws_dec_pass_payload()` holds a pointer into the WebSocket receive queue while passing a frame to the application's write callback. If that callback returns `CURL_WRITEFUNC_PAUSE`, the `PAUSE requested` trace invokes the debug callback before the WebSocket pass consumes those bytes (`CURLOPT_VERBOSE=1` and `curl_global_trace(\"write\")` enabled). Calling `curl_easy_pause(CURLPAUSE_RECV)` then `curl_easy_pause(CURLPAUSE_CONT)` there starts nested `ws_cw_flush()`. It frees the queued chunk before the outer pass reads it; in my curl 8.23.0 run, the PoC's client exits 0 after receiving all 200,005 bytes, but byte offset 32,756 differs from the server's stream, and ASan reports a 65,518-byte use-after-free read.\n\n### Affected version\n\n- curl 8.22.0 and 8.23.0 on Linux x86-64: reproduced; `b940cb1566` introduced this path in 8.22.0; attached ASan trace is from 8.22.0\n- Master at `629646d` (17 October 2026): same unguarded path confirmed by source inspection\n\nThis reports a different WebSocket queue from [report #4102061](https://hackerone.com/reports/4102061): applying the proposed `cw-out` and `cw-pause` guards from the still-open [PR #23431](https://github.com/curl/curl/pull/23431) leaves this PoC corrupting output; the attached diff adds regression test 2312.\n\nI used AI assistance to find and patch this; I reproduced the Release corruption and ASan UAF with the attached PoC.\n\n### Steps To Reproduce\n\n1. Check out `curl-8_23_0` and build static libcurl with WebSockets and verbose strings:\n\n   ```sh\n   cmake -S . -B bld-vuln -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DBUILD_TESTING=OFF -DBUILD_SHARED_LIBS=OFF -DCURL_USE_OPENSSL=ON -DENABLE_DEBUG=OFF -DCURL_DISABLE_VERBOSE_STRINGS=OFF\n   cmake --build bld-vuln --target libcurl_static -j2\n   ```\n2. Put the attached `client.c`, `server.py`, and `run.sh` in one directory; run `sh run.sh /absolute/path/to/curl/bld-vuln vulnerable` there to see the changed output\n3. Build the same tag with ASan and run `sh run.sh /absolute/path/to/curl/bld-asan asan`:\n\n   ```sh\n   cmake -S . -B bld-asan -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug -DBUILD_TESTING=OFF -DBUILD_SHARED_LIBS=OFF -DCURL_USE_OPENSSL=ON -DENABLE_DEBUG=OFF -DCURL_DISABLE_VERBOSE_STRINGS=OFF -DCMAKE_C_FLAGS=\"-fsanitize=address -fno-omit-frame-pointer -g\" -DCMAKE_EXE_LINKER_FLAGS=\"-fsanitize=address\"\n   cmake --build bld-asan --target libcurl_static -j2\n   ```\n\n4. Apply `ws-fix-and-test.diff`, configure and build with the Release flags from step 1 using `bld-fixed` instead of `bld-vuln`, then run `sh run.sh /absolute/path/to/curl/bld-fixed fixed` for the clean control\n\n## Impact\n\nA WebSocket server can supply the large frame, but the client must use the callback pattern above; libcurl can then deliver bytes from reused heap storage to that application's write callback, with no peer-visible leak or code execution demonstrated.",
